= 1I
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Number Theory}

= i
{parent=1i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Suppose the <prime numbers> were the finite list $p_1,\ldots,p_r$. The <integer> $N=p_1\cdots p_r+1>1$ has a <prime factor>, by repeatedly factoring any composite factor until a prime is reached. But division by any $p_j$ leaves remainder one, so that <prime factor> is absent from the list. This contradiction proves **there are infinitely many <prime numbers>**. The argument does not require $N$ itself to be prime.

= ii
{parent=1i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For any positive <integer> $m$, put $N=(m+1)!$. Each of $N+2,N+3,\ldots,N+m+1$ is composite, since $N+j$ is divisible by $j$ and larger than $j$. Let $p$ be the largest <prime number> at most $N+1$, and let $q$ be the smallest <prime number> at least $N+m+2$. Such $q$ exists by part (i), and no prime lies strictly between $p$ and $q$. Therefore these are consecutive <prime numbers> with
$$
\boxed{q-p\ge m+1.}
$$
Since $m$ is arbitrary, **gaps between consecutive <prime numbers> are unbounded**.

= 2F
{parent=Paper 4}
